S.append(grid([[P("Lvl", cellhead), P("Class", cellhead), P("What you get", cellhead), P("Where in the story", cellhead)]] + [[P(a, cellb), P(b, cell), P(c, cell), P(d_, cell)] for a, b, c, d_ in rows], [0.4*inch, 0.85*inch, 2.6*inch, W-3.85*inch]))

S.append(PageBreak()); S.append(P("Snapshots by saga, with the feats checked", h2)); S.append(rule())
S.append(P("Gods and Titans are statted by feel against the 5e Monster Manual's top end: Olympians as CR 22–26 with Legendary Resistance and Legendary Actions, Titans as Gargantuan CR 25–30 with a hundred-foot reach. The checks are honest about who finished whom.", small))
def snap(name, where, lvl, lines, checked):
    content = [P(name, snapname), P(where + "  ·  " + lvl, stattag)] + [P(l, snapline) for l in lines] + [P("<b>The feat, checked.</b> " + checked, snapline)]
    t = Table([[content]], colWidths=[W]); t.setStyle(TableStyle([("BACKGROUND",(0,0),(-1,-1),PARCH),("LINEBEFORE",(0,0),(0,-1),2.5,GREEN),("LEFTPADDING",(0,0),(-1,-1),10),("RIGHTPADDING",(0,0),(-1,-1),10),("TOPPADDING",(0,0),(-1,-1),7),("BOTTOMPADDING",(0,0),(-1,-1),7)]))
    return KeepTogether([t, Spacer(1,7)])
S.append(snap("The servant of Ares", "Ascension; Chains of Olympus · before the ten years end", "Levels 1–5 · Fighter 1–5", ["Str 17, Con 14 before the blood shows · <b>AC</b> 18 · <b>HP</b> 12 → 44 · the Blades of Chaos · two attacks from level 5"], "The Furies (Megaera, Alecto as a Charybdis, Tisiphone) are CR 13–16 fiends with illusions, and he kills all three; Orkos asks to die and he obliges, which is what breaks the oath. In Chains of Olympus Charon beats him once and sends him to Tartarus, and he comes back with the Gauntlet of Zeus and destroys him; Persephone dies; Atlas is chained to the Pillar of the World, not killed. A fifth-level fighter can't do any of it alone, and he doesn't: these are the god-armed years, with Olympus's magic loaned to him level by level (Poseidon's Rage, Zeus's Fury, the Efreet), which a DM hands out as one-use spell scrolls."))
S.append(snap("The God of War", "God of War (2005); Ghost of Sparta; Betrayal", "Levels 6–8 · Fighter 5–7 / Barbarian 1", ["<b>HP</b> 53 → 85 (Tough at level 7) · Rage · Know Your Enemy"], "Ares, a CR 26 god the size of a temple, dies on the Blade of the Gods: a weapon the size of a bridge, which in 5e is a one-use artifact that hits for 20d10 and needs a creature with Rage and Pandora's power behind it. He dies on the way there too (a pillar Ares throws) and climbs out of Hades, which is the first use of <b>Out of Hades</b>. As the God of War: Scylla, Erinys, Thanatos (CR 23) killed in a grief rage, his mother Callisto put down. Thanatos kills Deimos, not Kratos. In Betrayal he kills Ceryx; Argos's killer is someone else."))
S.append(snap("The war on Olympus", "God of War II and III", "Levels 9–13 · Fighter 8–11 / Barbarian 2", ["the demigod's Strength 26 and Constitution 22 by the war on Olympus · <b>HP</b> 95 → 188 · three attacks · Indomitable · the Blade of Olympus, the Claws of Hades, the Nemean Cestus, the Golden Fleece"], "The list, as the games give it. <b>Killed:</b> the Colossus of Rhodes, Theseus, Perseus, Euryale, Icarus (wings torn off), the Sisters of Fate, Poseidon (neck snapped), Hades (soul torn out with his own claws), Helios (head torn off), Hermes (legs cut off), Hercules (face beaten in with the Cestus), Hera (neck snapped), Hephaestus, Cronos (gutted with the Blade of Olympus), Perses, Gaia, and Zeus (impaled, then beaten to death with his fists). <b>Beaten, not killed:</b> Typhon, blinded with his own bow. <b>Someone else's:</b> Athena dies throwing herself on the blade. Against a CR 26 Olympian he needs his Rage, three attacks at +13 and Legendary-Resistance-burning maneuvers; against a Titan he needs to be climbing it, which Athletics +13 with advantage does. The last thing he does in Greece is run the Blade of Olympus through himself to give mankind Hope, and live."))
S.append(snap("Midgard", "God of War (2018)", "Levels 14–16 · Barbarian 3–5", ["<b>HP</b> 203 → 233 · the Leviathan Axe · the Guardian Shield · the World Tree"], "Baldur can't feel pain and can't be hurt by anything but mistletoe; three fights, and the third ends with Kratos snapping his neck. Magni dies to Kratos; Modi to Baldur. The nine Valkyries fall and their Queen, Sigrún, is freed, not killed. Freya fights him and lives. A Valkyrie Queen is a CR 22 flyer with Legendary Actions; Parry, a 2d8+16 axe, three attacks and Action Surge take her in about four rounds, which is about how long it takes in the game on Give Me God of War."))
S.append(snap("Ragnarök", "God of War Ragnarök", "Levels 17–19 · Barbarian 6–8", ["<b>HP</b> 248 → 278 · the Draupnir Spear · Branches of the Tree · Feral Instinct"], "Thor (CR 25, lightning and Mjölnir) fights him twice: a stalemate at the door in which Kratos knocks out a tooth, and a defeat in which Kratos spares him; it's Odin who kills Thor. Heimdall, who reads every move before it's made, loses his arm and then his breath to Kratos's hands. Odin is beaten by Kratos, Atreus and Freya together; Atreus traps him in a marble and Sindri crushes it. Gná falls to Kratos and Freya takes her head at her own request. The resistance to lightning is how he walks through Thor's bolts; the sheet still says he couldn't have done Odin alone, and neither does the game."))
S.append(snap("Valhalla", "the epilogue · after Ragnarök", "Level 20 · Fighter 11 / Barbarian 9", ["The headline block: AC 20, 293 HP, Legacy, the runic attacks, Out of Hades"], "Týr's trials are four fights against the old god of war in four armors (a hoplite, an Aztec, an Egyptian, a samurai), and Týr walks away from each; nobody dies in Valhalla, and the einherjar Kratos cuts down come back. The last fight isn't one: it's a talk with his younger self on Ares's throne. In the arena on these sheets, the thing he does that nobody else can is Anderson: the Blades keep the Paladin burning so nothing grows back, and then he does what Anderson's own sheet allows, the grapple and the heart. He wins that one about nine times in ten. He beats V, Jin, Guts and Crane, splits Talion, and loses to the Slayer, Link, Battle Beast, Guy and Smasher's guns."))

S.append(P("A god of hope", h2)); S.append(rule())
S.append(P("Ragnarök ends with Faye's last mural: a golden Kratos in Greek dress with the Leviathan Axe, and people kneeling to him. Valhalla ends with him on the throne, at peace with it, joining Freya's new pantheon; the game's own trophy for it is called <i>God of Hope</i>. Outlets disagree about whether he takes the title of god of war again or something gentler, and the sheet doesn't settle it: he is the God of War who decided war wasn't the point."))
S.append(KeepTogether([P("A DM's dial", h2), rule(), P("Three knobs. Out of Hades is canon three times over, but it's the most generous line on the sheet; drop it if the table wants him mortal-feeling. Legacy's extra radiant on every hit is what lets him outfight regenerators; Fury (fists) is the humbler Spartan Rage if the Blade of Olympus feels like too much. And the runic attacks recharge on a 5–6; make them once a short rest for a slower, more deliberate Kratos, which is also the most Norse one.")]))
doc.build(S, onFirstPage=on_page, onLaterPages=on_page); print("ok")
